## What I added

A new test case in `tests/unit/util.test.ts`, in the existing **"shell line parsing"** describe block:

```ts
test('a backslash-escaped operator still escalates (conservative over-match)', () => {
  const plan = parseShellLine('cat a \\| wc');
  assert.equal(plan.kind, 'compound', `got kind ${plan.kind}`);
  if (plan.kind === 'compound') {
    assert.ok(plan.operators.includes('pipe'), `operators: ${plan.operators.join(', ')}`);
    assert.deepEqual(plan.argv, ['bash', '-lc', 'cat a \\| wc']);
  }
});
```

**The edge case:** In a real POSIX shell, `cat a \| wc` is **not** a pipeline — the `\|` is a literal pipe argument to `cat`. But this kernel's `parseShellLine` consumes the backslash escape in `tokenizeShell` while leaving the resulting token `bare`, so `detectOperators` then flags the `|` as a pipe operator and escalates the command to an explicit `bash -lc` shell. This is a deliberate, security-safe over-escalation: the whole point of the shell-parse module (per its own doc comment) is that shell involvement is always *visible* in the plan, approval prompt, and audit event — never silently hidden. Writing it into a test locks in that behavior so a future refactor of the tokenizer or operator detector can't accidentally stop escalating escaped operators (which would silently reinterpret an escaped `\|` as a real pipe).

**Confirmation:** Ran `node --test --experimental-strip-types tests/unit/util.test.ts` — all **35 tests pass** (10 suites), exit code 0, including the new test.

